# SHA256SUMS -- provenance tripwire for the engine_getPayload golden corpus
# (P2 Task 1).
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# --------------------
# The same reason as golden/engine/SHA256SUMS: the corpus-derived tests only
# have judging power while these bytes are op-geth's output and not this
# repository's own. A silent refresh of a golden would otherwise pass. The
# sums are the machine-checked half of the op-geth pin
# e8800cffe53d459cde8a07c8e8f1de9d86e79e07 (v1.101702.2), the same pin the
# rest of golden/engine/ is generated from.
#
# WHAT IT DOES AND DOES NOT PROVE
# -------------------------------
# It proves: these 9 envelopes have not changed since the values were generated
# from the pinned op-geth through the real engine API path (ForkchoiceUpdated
# -> GetPayloadV<2|3|4|5>), whose determinism was established by two
# consecutive byte-identical runs per cell.
# It does NOT prove: that a regenerator who also refreshes this file used
# op-geth. Updating SHA256SUMS is a deliberate, reviewable act.
#
# REGENERATING (per cell; see manifest.txt for the case/fork mapping)
# ------------------------------------------------------------------
#   cd opstack-executor/tests/t8n
#   <opt8n-ref> --input cases/<case>.in.json \
#     --output golden/engine/getpayload/<fork>_v<version>.json \
#     --engine-getpayload [--engine-fork karst]
#   (opt8n-ref is generator/ built inside the op-geth pin tree; Task 4 wires
#   this into generator/regen.sh.)
# Verify from a shell with:
#   grep -v '^#' SHA256SUMS | shasum -a 256 -c
#
# Known data point (not a defect): karst_v5.json and jovian_v4.json have equal
# sums because the pinned op-geth returns the same envelope bytes for this
# input under V4 and V5; see manifest.txt.
